# Excluded from the build context — the snapshot Docker uploads before building.
# Without this file the context is gigabytes and every build crawls.

# --- 1. Virtualenvs ---------------------------------------------------------
# A virtualenv holds a full Windows Python install: huge, and useless in a Linux
# image. pip rebuilds everything from requirements.txt inside the container.
# (The dot-prefixed spelling is already covered by the rule below; these are the
# plain ones.)
venv/
env/

# --- 2. Everything hidden ---------------------------------------------------
# A single pattern for version control, virtualenvs, caches, and editor and
# tooling state — nothing the build needs starts with a dot.
#
# Excluding .env here is deliberate: pydantic-settings loads it from the working
# directory, so baking one into the image would freeze development credentials
# into a distributable artifact. Production values are injected by
# docker-compose (env_file / environment), and real environment variables take
# precedence over any .env anyway.
.*

# --- 3. Python bytecode -----------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# --- 4. Not needed to run the API -------------------------------------------
test/
test_main.http
pytest.ini
assets/

# --- 5. Documentation and local notes ---------------------------------------
# Matched by pattern rather than listed by name, so it keeps holding as notes
# come and go. requirements.txt is the one .txt the build genuinely needs, so it
# is added back afterwards — in .dockerignore the LAST matching rule wins.
*.md
*.txt
!requirements.txt

# --- 6. Uploaded avatars ----------------------------------------------------
# These come from the `avatar_data` volume at runtime, not from the image.
# Copying them in would also mean they reappear on every redeploy.
app/static/avatars/

# --- 7. Docker's own files --------------------------------------------------
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
